  "subtitle": "The chess computer that felt your move and talked back",
  "why_scout_cares": "A consumer chess computer whose interaction model is the board itself: press a piece on a square and the machine feels your move — no coordinates, no typing — then announces its reply aloud through a speech ROM. The first pairing of a pressure-sensing board with voice, from the Chicago company that invented the commercial chess computer.",
